\pard\keep\keepn\widctlpar\s1\sb360\sa80\sl252\slmult1\cf1\kerning2\i\f0\fs40 The Great Gatsby\i0 : Race, Class and the Jazz Age\par

\pard\keep\keepn\widctlpar\s2\sb160\sa80\sl252\slmult1\fs32 Prof Michael Nowlin\par

\pard\widctlpar\cf0\f1\fs24 University of Victoria\par
\par
\i Gatsby\i0  seems unfortunately tainted by a racist caricature in chapter 4. When Gatsby is driving Nick across the bridge, into Manhattan and they're going over Blackwell's Island, and they're passed by a limousine: \par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 driven by a white chauffeur, in which sat three modish negros, two bucks and a girl. I laughed out loud as the yolks of their eyeballs rolled toward us in haughty rivalry. (67)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
The fuller context of this detail won't allow us to dismiss this as gratuitous. It follows this lyrical paen to a magical Manhattan that is immediately undercut by an image of death. Because there's a funeral procession going by, but not just any funeral procession. It's a funeral for an immigrant. The deceased person's friends are looking at Nick and Gatsby: \par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 The friends looked out at us with the tragic eyes and short upper lips of south-eastern Europe. (67) \par

\pard\widctlpar\par
And it's then that the African Americans pass them. And the scene is summed up by Nick thinking to himself:\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi720 "Anything can happen now that we've slid over this bridge," \par

\pard\widctlpar\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi720 Even Gatsby could happen, without any particular wonder. (67)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
Nick reflexively made analogous associations early in chapter3 when in the face of various rumors about Gatsby, he tells us: \par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 I would have accepted without question the information that Gatsby sprang from the swamps of Louisiana from the lower east side of New York. That was comprehensible. But young men didn't\endash at least in my provincial inexperience I believed they didn\rquote t\endash drift coolly out of nowhere and buy a palace on Long Island Sound. (50)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
Why is it more comprehensible to Nick that rather than being a German spy, for example, Gatsby came from the swamps of Louisiana or the Lower East Side? Which is to say it seems that he must originally have been either a poor migrant from the deep Jim Crow South, or a Jewish immigrant. The Lower East Side was associated with a large Jewish immigrant population. There seems to be a kind of personal mythology at work about the making of modern Americans here \endash  cosmopolitan New York being the crucible of opportunity. And maybe you get some kind of confirmation when the first person Gatsby introduces Nick to on their arrival in the city is Mister Wolfshiem, who is first described as \ldblquote a small, flat nosed Jew.\rdblquote  (68)\par
\par
We don't have to be this far into the novel, though, to see race as a factor in it. In the first chapter, Tom Buchanan expounds on a book called \ldblquote\i The Rise of the Colored Empires\i0 ,\rdblquote  about the possibility that \ldblquote the white race might be utterly submerged.\rdblquote  We can hardly take Tom very seriously though, as nobody else does. And in fact, Nick describes Tom as \ldblquote nibbling at the edge of stale ideas.\rdblquote  But such ideas were hardly stale when Fitzgerald wrote. Indeed, they're arguably alive and well now.\par
\par
Fitzgerald was satirically alluding to Lothrop Stoddard's book that came out in 1920 called \i The Rising Tide of Color Against White World Supremacy\i0 . It was in fact published by Scribners, which had published his own novel. Such books like Stoddards and others of the same kind that lent intellectual justification to the newly restrictive Immigration Act of 1924, which put quotas on immigration based on national origin. And south-eastern Europeans and Eastern European Jews, were specific targets. \i Gatsby\i0  doesn't take any unambiguous stand toward these tendencies, though it's unequivocal about Tom's intellectual shortcomings, so we're not to take Tom seriously.\par
\par
But it's less clear about Nick's attitudes \endash  especially the extent to which that unaffected scorn that he instinctively feels towards Gatsby, and the likes of Gatsby, might be tainted by prejudices against the very mixed company that Gatsby keeps. Gatsby's connection to organized crime \endash  and notably a Jewish gangster \endash  is damning enough. But is it also damning that his parties are frequented by jazz musicians, Broadway performers? Gatsby is thus being associated with an African American musical form that was being commercialized and adapted by Jewish American composers in particular. And of course he's also associated with an entertainment industry that was increasingly being dominated by Jewish Americans.\par
\par
Jazz in the novel is epitomized by the rather comically named Vladimir Tostoff, the name was originally Les Epstein in the earlier version. It's tempting though to wonder, speculate a bit about those three modish negros going over the bridge. Could they could they be jazz musicians, for example, heading toward Harlem where cabarets like The Cotton Club were just beginning to thrive? They could also be performers in the hit musical \i Shuffle Along\i0 , the hit musical of 1921, and it played well into the summer of 1922. The jazz music, the Broadway songs, dances and movies were as foundational to the cultural mood of The Jazz Age as the Prohibition that was making gangsters rich. These were the vehicles for upward mobility for African Americans after the great migration Northward during World War One \endash  and also for Jewish Americans and first and second generation immigrants.\par
\par
Unless it makes sense, I think, for Fitzgerald to want to associate Gatsby, with his suddenly made fortune, his sudden proximity to the Buchanan's house with the threat of a rising tide of color, though it might be more aptly described as the rising tide of a modern American popular culture that is blacker than it is white, that is more culturally pluralistic than Anglo Saxon, to use one of the terms of the day. More irreverent than genteel. More democratic than elite.\par
\par
Among those decrying it was the automobile manufacturer, Henry Ford who, in a collection of anti-Semitic screeds in 1921 (\lquote Jewish Jazz \endash  moron Music \endash  Becomes Our National Music\rquote ) decried the fact that Jewish Jazz had become America's national music. Such an association of Gatsby with what we call racial otherness hardly supports the argument, an argument that has found some currency, though it's dismissed by most Fitzgerald scholars: that Gatsby is in fact a black man, passing as white. I think there's no evidence, external evidence to support such a claim and Fitzgerald's initial decision to name the novel \i Trimalchio\i0  \endash  again, Trimalchio is a former Roman slave, I think can be more plausibly explained by his emulation of recent titles like \lquote The Waste Land\rquote  and \i Ulysses\i0 . But that association, the mere association, with blackness and racial evidence, I think it ia significant in itself. And it's Tom who makes it most explicitly when he declares in the argument scene in chapter 7: \par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 I suppose the latest thing is to sit back and let Mr Nobody from Nowhere make love to your wife [\'85] Nowadays, people begin sneering at family life and family institutions. And next, they'll throw everything overboard and have intermarriage between black and white. (123 \endash  124)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
So for Tom, there's a slippery slope in effect from relaxing sexual mores, collapsing class barriers, which enough money might make Mr Nobody from Nowhere his equal and, the worst horror of all, miscegenation \endash  or tolerance of miscegenation. Yet Gatsby only presages, in Tom's thinking, the acceptance of miscegenation. He isn't assumed to be black himself. And yet the threat of miscegenation is sounded earlier through a pointed allusion. When Jordan Baker finishes telling her story about Gatsby's background, the backstory about his relationship with Daisy, telling this to Nick, they overhear some young girls in the park singing the popular 1921 song called \lquote The Sheik of Araby\rquote . That song capitalized on the popular 1921 silent film, \i The Sheik\i0 , which starred, and made a star of, the Italian born American Rudolph Valentino, whose skin tone in the film is decidedly darker than the woman into whose tent creeps. Again though, the allusion and other associations don't cryptically identify Gatsby as black so much as they associate him with a blacker, ethnically mixed, sexually suggestive popular American culture. Its sights and sounds are the backdrop and soundtrack to the novel. Its effects are felt as early as the post-war period when Daisy Buchanan gets back to her social routine in Louisville after Gatsby has left and before she meets Tom, we're told:\par
\par
All night the saxophones wailed the hopeless comment of the \lquote Beale Street Blues\rquote  while a hundred pairs of golden and silver slippers shuffled in the silver dust (143)\par
\par
So, anxieties about race in the novel, which are there, shouldn't cause us to forget that \i Gatsby\i0  is, first and foremost, about a class interloper. And yet it also shows us how class differences in America tend to get racialized. That threats to an entrenched class system are best displaced onto immigrants and upstart blacks, what Tony Morrison would call the Africanist presence in this novel, for example, like those three new modish negros crossing the bridge, that pale yellow negro who's one of the witnesses of the car accident. That figure, that black presence makes for a kind of wry comment on the impasses of this dream of white freedom and that white freedom and self-making that \i Gatsby\i0 , like so much classic American literature, is wrestling with. \par
\par
And the conclusion of the novel suggests there's no real social threat posed by a demographically more diverse America to the world of East Egg. It's still separated by this body of water from West Egg. Mr Nobody from Nowhere is killed for his hubris. Careless plutocrats like the Buchanans, as Nick recognizes: \par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720 smashed up things and creatures and then retreated back into their money or their vast carelessness. (170)\par

\pard\widctlpar\par
So ,some things don't change, but the novel does seem to take for granted that a new style of music \ldblquote one part American and three parts American Negro\rdblquote  as Joel Rogers put it in the 1925 classic book, \i The New Negro\i0 , that seems to have conquered.\par
\par

